<%- include('partials/head') %>

<div class="hero">
  <h1>No bot gets in without a human saying yes.</h1>
  <p>
    BotApprove removes every bot that joins your server and holds it for review. It does not
    matter who invited it. The owner gets no exception, because a compromised owner account is
    exactly how servers get wiped.
  </p>
  <div class="cta">
    <a class="btn primary" href="<%= inviteUrl %>">Add to Discord</a>
    <a class="btn" href="/login">Sign in to the dashboard</a>
  </div>
  <p class="hint">
    Free to add, open source, with no trial countdown. Signing in asks for your identity and server list,
    nothing else.
  </p>
</div>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>What happens when a bot joins</h2></div>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>It is kicked first</strong>
        <span>Removal happens before any audit log lookup or image download, so nothing sits
          inside your server while BotApprove thinks about it.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Approvers get the facts</strong>
        <span>Account age, who invited it from the audit log, the permissions its role actually
          held after joining, and whether its avatar looks reused.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Nothing returns without a decision</strong>
        <span>Approving adds it to the whitelist. It is never re-invited automatically. Someone
          has to do that by hand.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>How it fails</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    Every failure mode resolves the same way: toward not trusting the bot. The marker shows
    whether the outcome is safe on its own or needs a human.
  </p>
  <dl class="modes">
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Something errors</dt>
        <dd>The bot is kicked. A failed check is never mistaken for a passed one.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Name matches your blocklist</dt>
        <dd>Stopped before the whitelist is read, so a stolen approval cannot be reused.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Bot carries a verified badge</dt>
        <dd>Shown to the approver, never trusted. Verified apps get compromised too.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Subscription lapses</dt>
        <dd>The gate keeps running. Screening is free forever and the bot never leaves.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode alert">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Someone weakens BotApprove</dt>
        <dd>Losing a permission it had, or being moved down the role list, is reported as a
          probable attack rather than a settings change.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode crit">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>A kick fails</dt>
        <dd>Treated as critical and escalated. It means an unapproved bot is in your server
          right now.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>Open source</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    BotApprove asks for permission to kick and ban people in your server. You should not have to
    take anyone's word for what it does with that. Everything above is on GitHub under AGPL-3.0:
    the join pipeline, the permission checks, the database schema, and the tests holding them in
    place.
  </p>
  <dl class="modes">
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Read the part that decides</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>src/services/botJoinPipeline.js</code> is the whole gate, in the order it runs.
          Every claim on this page is a few hundred lines you can check.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Run it yourself</dt>
        <dd>
          Node and a SQLite file, no external services. If you would rather not trust a hosted
          bot with your server, host your own.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Changes have to be published</dt>
        <dd>
          AGPL means anyone running a modified BotApprove as a service has to publish what they
          changed. A quietly weakened fork cannot stay quiet.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Report a flaw privately</dt>
        <dd>
          Security reports go to the operator directly, not into a public issue where they would
          be a working exploit against every server until a fix ships.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <% if (repoUrl) { %>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn with-icon" href="<%= repoUrl %>" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true" fill="currentColor">
          <path d="M6.766 11.328c-2.063-.25-3.516-1.734-3.516-3.656 0-.781.281-1.625.75-2.188-.203-.515-.172-1.609.063-2.062.625-.078 1.468.25 1.968.703.594-.187 1.219-.281 1.985-.281.765 0 1.39.094 1.953.265.484-.437 1.344-.765 1.969-.687.218.422.25 1.515.046 2.047.5.593.766 1.39.766 2.203 0 1.922-1.453 3.375-3.547 3.64.531.344.89 1.094.89 1.954v1.625c0 .468.391.734.86.547C13.781 14.359 16 11.53 16 8.03 16 3.61 12.406 0 7.984 0 3.563 0 0 3.61 0 8.031a7.88 7.88 0 0 0 5.172 7.422c.422.156.828-.125.828-.547v-1.25c-.219.094-.5.156-.75.156-1.031 0-1.64-.562-2.078-1.609-.172-.422-.36-.672-.719-.719-.187-.015-.25-.093-.25-.187 0-.188.313-.328.625-.328.453 0 .844.281 1.25.86.313.452.64.655 1.031.655s.641-.14 1-.5c.266-.265.47-.5.657-.656"/>
        </svg>
        Read the source
      </a>
    </div>
  <% } %>
</section>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>Pricing</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The approval gate is free forever, with no trial countdown. Premium adds threat intelligence
    and automation for <%= price.symbol %><%= price.amount %> per month, per server.
  </p>
  <a class="btn" href="/pricing">See what premium adds</a>
</section>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>Add it</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    BotApprove asks for the permissions it actually uses and nothing more. It never requests the
    Message Content intent, so it cannot read your messages.
  </p>
  <dl class="modes">
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Kick Members, Ban Members</dt>
        <dd>Removing an unapproved bot, and banning one on the shared threat list.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>View Audit Log</dt>
        <dd>Reading who invited a bot. This is the only way to know after the fact.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>View Messages, Send Messages, Embed Links, Attach Files</dt>
        <dd>Posting the approval card in your chosen channel, with the bot's avatar attached.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Read Message History, Change Nickname</dt>
        <dd>Editing its own approval message after a restart, and wearing the name you pick.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dl>
  <div class="actions">
    <a class="btn primary" href="<%= inviteUrl %>">Add to Discord</a>
    <% if (supportUrl) { %>
      <a class="btn" href="<%= supportUrl %>" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">Support server</a>
    <% } %>
  </div>
  <p class="hint">
    After adding it, drag the BotApprove role above where new bots land, then run
    <code>/config notify-channel</code> to pick where approvals appear.<% if (supportUrl) { %>
    Stuck on any of that, or want a bot added to the shared threat list? Ask in the support
    server.<% } %>
  </p>
</section>

<%- include('partials/foot') %>
